 if they are equal when sorting the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"scalars in ascending order by their combining class."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"For example, consider the sequence "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"\"\\u{0041}\\u{0301}\\u{0316}\""},{"type":"text","text":" (LATIN"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"CAPITAL LETTER A, COMBINING ACUTE ACCENT, COMBINING GRAVE ACCENT BELOW)."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"The combining classes of these scalars have the numeric values 0, 230, and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"220, respectively. Sorting these scalars by their combining classes yields"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"\"\\u{0041}\\u{0316}\\u{0301}\"","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", so two strings that differ only by the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"ordering of those scalars would compare as equal:"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift","code":["let aboveBeforeBelow = \"\\u{0041}\\u{0301}\\u{0316}\"","let belowBeforeAbove = \"\\u{0041}\\u{0316}\\u{0301}\"","print(aboveBeforeBelow == belowBeforeAbove)","\/\/ Prints \"true\""]},{"type":"heading","text":"Named and Unnamed Combining Classes","anchor":"Named-and-Unnamed-Combining-Classes","level":1},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Canonical combining classes are defined in the Unicode Standard as"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"integers in the range ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0...254"},{"type":"text","text":". For convenience, the standard assigns"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"symbolic names to a subset of these combining classes."}]},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"CanonicalCombiningClass"},{"type":"text","text":" type conforms to "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"RawRepresentable"},{"type":"text","text":" with a"},{"type":"t